# ==========================================================================
# .dockerignore — a WHITELIST for the runtime-only Dockerfile
# ==========================================================================
#
# The image is built from what the host's `next build` produced (see
# Dockerfile), so the context is exactly three folders and nothing else:
# no source, no node_modules (the standalone folder carries its own traced
# copy), no photographic masters, no .git. That keeps the context small —
# it used to be the whole 158 MB working tree — and keeps secrets out of it.
#
# Order matters: `*` excludes everything, the `!` lines bring the three
# folders back, and the lines after them take out what the standalone
# tracer copies in but an image must never contain.

*
!.next/standalone
!.next/static
!public

# The tracer copies the whole checkout into .next/standalone. These would
# bake a secret (.env), a stale snapshot of uploads and invoices (media/,
# private/ — the live ones are volumes), or dead weight into the image.
.next/standalone/.env*
.next/standalone/media
.next/standalone/private
.next/standalone/assets
.next/standalone/docs
.next/standalone/tests
.next/standalone/.git
.next/standalone/.claude
.next/standalone/*.tsbuildinfo
.next/standalone/.next/cache

**/.DS_Store
